WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.660
Two learners each score seven points, and three learners

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.910
each score nine points.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.650
What is the mean score for all five learners?

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.350
Find the totals.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.950
The first group scores two times seven: fourteen points.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.080
The second group scores three times

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.730
nine: twenty-seven points.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.720
Together that is forty-one points for five learners.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.460
Forty-one divided by five is eight point two.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.860
The mean score is eight point two.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.210
Why is the mean above eight,

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.290
halfway between seven and nine?

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.450
Three learners score nine and two score seven,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.990
so the balance point is pulled towards nine.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.100
If you add one extra score of nine to four scores

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.060
averaging eight, the mean moves one fifth of the way

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.150
from eight to nine.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.060
If the groups were the same size, two learners at seven

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.090
and two at nine, the mean would be exactly eight.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.170
The two group means now carry equal weight,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:58.930
so their simple average works.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.740
Combine the totals, then divide by the total

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:03.770
number of people.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.700
Rebuild this, then find the mean score when four

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.750
learners score five each and one learner scores ten.
